<Essay
  docNo="03"
  title="How a product creates value"
  description="Whether a product's value is fixed at ship: the problem behind a request, the hardware that sets what can grow, and what use teaches the next generation."
>
  <div class="sub">
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p class="opener">Once a product ships, is its value fixed?</p>
      <p>
        Software can change after launch. Hardware mostly cannot. Much of a product's future value
        is decided before it ships. The rest is learned from use.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.1</span>
      <h2>A request is only the beginning</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Customers rarely arrive with requirements. They ask for a feature, and a feature is already
        one interpretation of the problem.
      </p>
      <p>
        "I want a sensor on that window" sounds complete. Often the real obstacle is that Wi-Fi does
        not reach the window, and nobody wants another hub. Ring's 2026 sensors run on Amazon
        Sidewalk, with no hub or base station. They answer the problem behind the request.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        Ring Sensors, announced at CES 2026.
        <a
          href="https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/devices/amazon-ces-2026-fire-tv-ring-alexa-plus"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Reference ↗</a
        >
      </p>
      <div class="pullquote">
        <p>The request is the beginning. The problem behind it is the work.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.2</span>
      <h2>Hardware sets what can grow</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        The sensor, processor, radio, battery and enclosure are fixed at ship. Together they set the
        ceiling for every update that follows.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fire Watch, announced at CES 2026, looks for smoke and fire in video from cameras already
        installed. A feature like that can only arrive later if the hardware in the field can
        already carry it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where the hardware meets a limit, an accessory can extend it. A battery camera mounted far
        from an outlet runs only as long as its charge. A solar panel removes that limit. Later
        panels deliver up to twice the power and need less sunlight.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        Fire Watch:
        <a
          href="https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/devices/amazon-ces-2026-fire-tv-ring-alexa-plus"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Reference ↗</a
        >. Super Solar Panel:
        <a href="https://ring.com/products/super-solar-panel" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          >Reference ↗</a
        >
      </p>
      <p><strong>Headroom costs money today. It buys value later.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.3</span>
      <h2>Use continues the conversation</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        A return is the most direct message a customer sends. Read one at a time, a return is a
        failure. Read together, returns show a pattern: the use the specification did not foresee.
      </p>
      <p>
        Many returned units test fine. The product met its spec. The spec did not meet the use.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sustaining closes the loop. It corrects the product in the field, and the next generation's
        requirements start from what the field taught.
      </p>
      <div class="pullquote">
        <p>Value is not fixed at ship. It is what the product keeps solving.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Essay>
